{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python3 read_census.py | tail -7'","aggregated_output":"\nDraws: 3 (0 census, 3 July at seq 148 only).\nRequired links right: 3; missed: 3 [('july-linked-1', '148:v8'), ('july-linked-2', '148:v8'), ('july-linked-3', '148:v8')]\nWrong links: 0 []\nFragile (margin below 0.10 on a linked or required pair): 6 [('july-linked-1', '148:v9', 0.05), ('july-linked-2', '148:v9', 0.09), ('july-linked-3', '148:v9', 0.03), ('july-linked-1', '148:v8', 0.06), ('july-linked-2', '148:v8', 0.07), ('july-linked-3', '148:v8', 0.03)]\n\nGate (no wrong link in any draw; v9 reads v14 in every draw): PASS\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python3 -c 'import sqlite3,json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nroot=Path(\\\"../../..\\\").resolve()\np=root/\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-2/prod/views.sqlite\\\"\nc=sqlite3.connect(p.as_uri()+\\\"?mode=ro&immutable=1\\\",uri=True)\nc.row_factory=sqlite3.Row\nfor v in (\\\"v1\\\",\\\"v2\\\",\\\"v8\\\",\\\"v9\\\",\\\"v14\\\",\\\"v15\\\",\\\"v16\\\",\\\"v17\\\",\\\"v21\\\",\\\"v28\\\",\\\"v29\\\",\\\"v32\\\",\\\"v33\\\",\\\"v35\\\",\\\"v36\\\"):\n r=c.execute(\\\"select v.*,k.measure,k.scope,k.period_start,k.period_end,k.basis,k.unit from views v join view_keys k using (view_id) where view_id=?\\\",(v,)).fetchone()\n print(v,r[\\\"created_seq\\\"],r[\\\"question\\\"],\\\"KEY\\\",tuple(r[k] for k in (\\\"measure\\\",\\\"scope\\\",\\\"period_start\\\",\\\"period_end\\\",\\\"basis\\\",\\\"unit\\\")))\n raw=c.execute(\\\"select handover_json from handovers where seq=?\\\",(r[\\\"created_seq\\\"],)).fetchone()[0]\n h=json.loads(raw)\n candidates=[ca for ca in h[\\\"candidates\\\"] if ca[\\\"question\\\"]==r[\\\"question\\\"]]\n for ca in candidates:\n  for e in h[\\\"evidence\\\"]:\n   if e[\\\"id\\\"] in ca[\\\"evidence_ids\\\"]: print(\\\" \\\",e[\\\"quote\\\"][:370])\n print(\\\" \\\",c.execute(\\\"select group_concat(condition,\\\\x27 | \\\\x27) from rungs where view_id=?\\\",(v,)).fetchone()[0])\nprint(\\\"old-2 count match_records\\\",c.execute(\\\"select count(*) from match_records\\\").fetchone()[0])\nprint(\\\"old-2 prefix count match_records\\\",c.execute(\\\"select count(*) from match_records where seq<=147\\\").fetchone()[0])'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python3 -c 'import sqlite3,json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nroot=Path(\\\"../../..\\\").resolve()\np=root/\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-2/prod/views.sqlite\\\"\nc=sqlite3.connect(p.as_uri()+\\\"?mode=ro&immutable=1\\\",uri=True)\nc.row_factory=sqlite3.Row\nfor v in (\\\"v1\\\",\\\"v2\\\",\\\"v8\\\",\\\"v9\\\",\\\"v14\\\",\\\"v15\\\",\\\"v16\\\",\\\"v17\\\",\\\"v21\\\",\\\"v28\\\",\\\"v29\\\",\\\"v32\\\",\\\"v33\\\",\\\"v35\\\",\\\"v36\\\"):\n r=c.execute(\\\"select v.*,k.measure,k.scope,k.period_start,k.period_end,k.basis,k.unit from views v join view_keys k using (view_id) where view_id=?\\\",(v,)).fetchone()\n print(v,r[\\\"created_seq\\\"],r[\\\"question\\\"],\\\"KEY\\\",tuple(r[k] for k in (\\\"measure\\\",\\\"scope\\\",\\\"period_start\\\",\\\"period_end\\\",\\\"basis\\\",\\\"unit\\\")))\n raw=c.execute(\\\"select handover_json from handovers where seq=?\\\",(r[\\\"created_seq\\\"],)).fetchone()[0]\n h=json.loads(raw)\n candidates=[ca for ca in h[\\\"candidates\\\"] if ca[\\\"question\\\"]==r[\\\"question\\\"]]\n for ca in candidates:\n  for e in h[\\\"evidence\\\"]:\n   if e[\\\"id\\\"] in ca[\\\"evidence_ids\\\"]: print(\\\" \\\",e[\\\"quote\\\"][:370])\n print(\\\" \\\",c.execute(\\\"select group_concat(condition,\\\\x27 | \\\\x27) from rungs where view_id=?\\\",(v,)).fetchone()[0])\nprint(\\\"old-2 count match_records\\\",c.execute(\\\"select count(*) from match_records\\\").fetchone()[0])\nprint(\\\"old-2 prefix count match_records\\\",c.execute(\\\"select count(*) from match_records where seq<=147\\\").fetchone()[0])'\"","aggregated_output":"v1 1 How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed under its AI-cloud model by the publication of its FY2027 annual report? KEY ('financing_amount', 'AI-cloud model credit support', '2026-02-01', '2027-01-31', 'n/a', 'USD')\n  This new model enables AI clouds to procure NVIDIA infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise and ISV customers through economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model. Through the partnership, AI clouds will sell NVIDIA-powered cloud services, with NVIDIA earning both standard product revenue and a share of the cloud revenue on the supported capacity\n  Committed credit support is below $1 billion. | Committed credit support is at least $1 billion and below $5 billion. | Committed credit support is at least $5 billion and below $10 billion. | Committed credit support is at least $10 billion and below $20 billion. | Committed credit support is at least $20 billion.\nv2 8 At NVIDIA's FY2027 annual report, how much will its commitments for customer and cloud-provider credit support, guarantees, and capacity backstops reach? KEY ('financing_amount', 'NVIDIA guarantees, credit support, and capacity backstops for customers and cloud providers', '2026-02-01', '2027-01-31', 'n/a', 'USD')\n  On July 1, according to the technology news outlet The Information, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young cloud service providers that lease or rent its GPUs—if these companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent their computing capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price.\n  On July 1, NVIDIA published a statement on its official website announcing a new business model that combines revenue sharing with credit support, enabling AI cloud providers to procure NVIDIA infrastructure without bearing the full upfront capital expenditure, and allowing them to offer computing services to downstream AI-native enterprises, model developers, and cor\n  According to a regulatory filing submitted by NVIDIA in May of this year (covering the quarter ended in April), NVIDIA subsequently added another $3.5 billion to guarantee customer data center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares.\n  Total commitments are less than $2 billion. | Total commitments are at least $2 billion and less than $5 billion. | Total commitments are at least $5 billion and less than $10 billion. | Total commitments are at least $10 billion and less than $20 billion. | Total commitments are at least $20 billion.\nv8 49 How much revenue will NVIDIA generate from stand-alone CPU sales in FY2027? KEY ('other', 'NVIDIA stand-alone CPU', '2026-02-01', '2027-01-31', 'n/a', 'USD')\n  Intel has led here for years and holds more than 59% of total CPU market share\n  AMD has gained ground, progressing from a low of about 17% back in 2016 to 38% today.\n  Nvidia already forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year.\n  Stand-alone CPU sales exceed $25 billion in FY2027. | Stand-alone CPU sales are at least $20 billion and no more than $25 billion in FY2027. | Stand-alone CPU sales are at least $15 billion and less than $20 billion in FY2027. | Stand-alone CPU sales are at least $10 billion and less than $15 billion in FY2027. | Stand-alone CPU sales are less than $10 billion in FY2027.\nv9 62 How much Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027? KEY ('segment_revenue', 'Data Center', '2026-02-01', '2027-01-31', 'GAAP', 'USD')\n  During the first quarter of fiscal 2027, the Data Center segment generated $75.25 billion in revenues, accounting for 92% of total sales. Revenues from the segment surged 92% year over year and grew 21% sequentially, driven by the rapid ramp-up of Blackwell 300 systems and robust networking demand.\n  FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $450 billion. | FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $400 billion and below $450 billion. | FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $350 billion and below $400 billion. | FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $300 billion and below $350 billion. | FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is below $300 billion.\nv14 148 How much total revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027? KEY ('total_revenue', '', '2026-02-01', '2027-01-31', 'n/a', 'USD')\n  The chipmaker is projected to deliver $228 billion in profits on sales of $393 billion in fiscal 2027, which ends Jan. 31. That would represent an expansion of 90% and 82%, respectively, according to data compiled by Bloomberg.\n  Total revenue is at least $450 billion. | Total revenue is at least $400 billion but less than $450 billion. | Total revenue is at least $350 billion but less than $400 billion. | Total revenue is at least $300 billion but less than $350 billion. | Total revenue is less than $300 billion.\nv15 206 How much will NVIDIA's supply commitments reach at the publication of its FY2027 annual report? KEY ('financing_amount', 'NVIDIA supply commitments', '2026-02-01', '2027-01-31', 'n/a', 'USD')\n  NVIDIA, by contrast, carries $119 billion in supply commitments, tying its fortunes to hyperscaler order books .\n  NVIDIA's supply commitments are below $50 billion. | NVIDIA's supply commitments are at least $50 billion and below $100 billion. | NVIDIA's supply commitments are at least $100 billion and below $150 billion. | NVIDIA's supply commitments are at least $150 billion and below $200 billion. | NVIDIA's supply commitments are at least $200 billion.\nv16 265 How much total CPU revenue will NVIDIA generate in FY2027? KEY ('product_revenue', 'total CPU', '2026-02-01', '2027-01-31', 'n/a', 'USD')\n  opens a brand new $200 billion TAM for NVIDIA, a market we have never addressed before.\n  visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue this year.\n  Total CPU revenue is greater than $25 billion. | Total CPU revenue is greater than $20 billion and at most $25 billion. | Total CPU revenue is at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion. | Total CPU revenue is at least $10 billion and less than $15 billion. | Total CPU revenue is less than $10 billion.\nv17 321 How much will NVIDIA's total revenue grow in FY2028? KEY ('growth_rate', 'total revenue', '2027-02-01', '2028-01-31', 'n/a', 'percent')\n  they also predict growth slowing to a 41% revenue increase for fiscal 2028.\n  FY2028 total revenue growth is at least 60%. | FY2028 total revenue growth is at least 50% but less than 60%. | FY2028 total revenue growth is at least 35% but less than 50%. | FY2028 total revenue growth is at least 20% but less than 35%. | FY2028 total revenue growth is less than 20%.\nv21 534 How much will NVIDIA's total revenue grow in FY2027? KEY ('growth_rate', 'total revenue', '2026-02-01', '2027-01-31', 'n/a', 'percent')\n  By the end of fiscal year 2027 (ending August 2027), Wall Street expects an additional 84% growth for Micron, while it estimates only 42% for Nvidia.\n  FY2027 total revenue growth is at least 60%. | FY2027 total revenue growth is at least 50% and below 60%. | FY2027 total revenue growth is at least 35% and below 50%, bracketing Wall Street's 42% estimate cited in the item. | FY2027 total revenue growth is at least 20% and below 35%. | FY2027 total revenue growth is below 20%.\nv28 746 What gross margin will NVIDIA report for FY2027? KEY ('gross_margin', '', '2026-02-01', '2027-01-31', 'n/a', 'percent')\n  Gross margin, guided to the mid-seventies for fiscal 2027\n  FY2027 gross margin is at least 79%. | FY2027 gross margin is at least 76% and below 79%. | FY2027 gross margin is at least 73% and below 76%, around the mid-seventies guidance. | FY2027 gross margin is at least 70% and below 73%. | FY2027 gross margin is below 70%.\nv29 821 How much will NVIDIA's equity investment commitments reach at the publication of its FY2027 annual report? KEY ('financing_amount', 'Equity investment commitments to ecosystem partners', '2026-02-01', '2027-01-31', 'n/a', 'USD')\n  Nvidia has committed to directly providing $70 billion in equity investments into ecosystem partners, per new research from Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya.\n  \"Overall, we view Nvidia's $70 billion investments as easily manageable given its ability to generate as much as about $470 billion of free cash flow over just the next two years (2026-2027), or about 15% of free cash flow,\" Arya wrote.\n  Commitments are below $25 billion. | Commitments are at least $25 billion and below $50 billion. | Commitments are at least $50 billion and below $75 billion. | Commitments are at least $75 billion and below $100 billion. | Commitments are at least $100 billion.\nv32 1029 How much will NVIDIA’s committed payment to license Poolside’s AI technology reach at the publication of its FY2027 annual report? KEY ('financing_amount', 'Poolside AI-technology license payment commitment', '2026-02-01', '2027-01-31', 'n/a', 'USD')\n  The company will pay $6 billion to license Poolside’s AI technology\n  The disclosed commitment is less than $5 billion. | The disclosed commitment is at least $5 billion and less than $6 billion. | The disclosed commitment is at least $6 billion and less than $7 billion. | The disclosed commitment is at least $7 billion and less than $8 billion. | The disclosed commitment is at least $8 billion.\nv33 1102 What gross margin will NVIDIA report for FY2028? KEY ('gross_margin', '', '2027-02-01', '2028-01-31', 'GAAP', 'percent')\n  Gross margins, which were 75% in the second quarter, are projected to drop to 74% in Q3, bottom out at 71–72% in Q4 FY27, and settle at 72–73% in FY28 after Nvidia's price increases take effect in Q1.\n  Many of you have expressed concerns regarding our gross margins as component costs have risen significantly. As you are already aware, we are experiencing extreme pricing conditions in memory... As a result, we are resetting expectations today,\n  We expect margins to bottom in Q4 in the 71% to 72% range before settling at 72% to 73% in fiscal year '28, as executed price increases take effect in Q1,\n  FY2028 gross margin is at least 75%. | FY2028 gross margin is at least 73% and below 75%. | FY2028 gross margin is at least 71% and below 73%. | FY2028 gross margin is at least 69% and below 71%. | FY2028 gross margin is below 69%.\nv35 1238 How much total revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2028? KEY ('total_revenue', '', '2027-02-01', '2028-01-31', 'GAAP', 'USD')\n  Analysts see revenue reaching about $390 billion in 2027 and more than $550 billion in 2028, with annual sales growth of roughly 24% to 25%.\n  Management's outlook is even stronger: about 70% growth in fiscal 2028, which could put revenue near $670 billion.\n  FY2028 total revenue is at least $700 billion. | FY2028 total revenue is at least $600 billion and less than $700 billion. | FY2028 total revenue is at least $500 billion and less than $600 billion. | FY2028 total revenue is at least $400 billion and less than $500 billion. | FY2028 total revenue is less than $400 billion.\nv36 1262 How much will NVIDIA's committed investment in MediaTek through convertible bonds reach at the publication of its FY2027 annual report? KEY ('financing_amount', 'NVIDIA investment in MediaTek through convertible bonds', '2026-02-01', '2027-01-31', 'n/a', 'USD')\n  Nvidia announced on Monday that it is investing $3.5 billion in MediaTek through convertible bonds, deepening a partnership with the Taiwanese chipmaker that spans AI data center infrastructure, consumer PCs, and automotive platforms.\n  The reported commitment is less than $1.75 billion. | The reported commitment is at least $1.75 billion but less than $3.5 billion. | The reported commitment is exactly $3.5 billion. | The reported commitment is more than $3.5 billion but no more than $5.25 billion. | The reported commitment is more than $5.25 billion.\nold-2 count match_records 1287\nold-2 prefix count match_records 62\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba census-dry.log | tail -12; nl -ba ../../../scripts/replay.py | sed -n '49,69p;98,107p'; nl -ba ../../../AGENTS.md | sed -n '177,186p;335,359p'; nl -ba ../../DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '6459,6477p;10504,10516p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba census-dry.log | tail -12; nl -ba ../../../scripts/replay.py | sed -n '49,69p;98,107p'; nl -ba ../../../AGENTS.md | sed -n '177,186p;335,359p'; nl -ba ../../DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '6459,6477p;10504,10516p'\"","aggregated_output":"    55\t1106 eligible [] not eligible 1\n    56\t1238 eligible ['v17', 'v14', 'v21', 'v16', 'v9', 'v33', 'v8', 'v1', 'v15', 'v2', 'v28', 'v29', 'v32'] not eligible 47\n    57\t1262 eligible ['v1', 'v2', 'v15', 'v29', 'v32', 'v8', 'v9', 'v14', 'v16', 'v17', 'v21', 'v28', 'v33', 'v35'] not eligible 49\n    58\t1296 eligible [] not eligible 1\n    59\t1394 eligible [] not eligible 1\n    60\t1543 eligible [] not eligible 1\n    61\t1664 eligible [] not eligible 1\n    62\t1677 eligible [] not eligible 1\n    63\t1828 eligible [] not eligible 1\n    64\t1874 eligible [] not eligible 1\n    65\tlinks proposed by the fake answers: 105 complete: True []\n    66\texit 0\n    49\t\n    50\t\n    51\tdef refuse_harness_ledger(path):\n    52\t    \"\"\"A ledger marked harness-only (a `harness_only` table, D694's July test) is written by its harness alone; read-only inspection stays\n    53\t    open. Refused under any configuration, before any item is routed.\"\"\"\n    54\t    ledger = Path(path)\n    55\t    if not ledger.exists():\n    56\t        return\n    57\t    try:\n    58\t        with sqlite3.connect(ledger.resolve().as_uri() + '?mode=ro', uri=True) as conn:\n    59\t            marked = conn.execute(\"select count(*) from sqlite_master where type='table' and name='harness_only'\").fetchone()[0]\n    60\t    except sqlite3.Error as error:\n    61\t        raise SystemExit(f'cannot inspect existing ledger: {error}') from error\n    62\t    if marked:\n    63\t        raise SystemExit('this ledger is marked harness-only; an ordinary replay does not write it')\n    64\t\n    65\t\n    66\tdef refuse_links_engine(cfg):\n    67\t    \"\"\"D694: poc-v119's matching engine runs only in its July test harness in this version, not from an ordinary launcher.\"\"\"\n    68\t    if cfg.matching.engine == 'jev-links':\n    69\t        raise SystemExit(f'config {cfg.version}: matching engine jev-links is not a product entry point in this version (D694)')\n    98\t    cfg = config.load(args.config)\n    99\t    if cfg.baseline is not None:\n   100\t        raise SystemExit(f'config {cfg.version} uses retired seeded-question baseline; select a configuration without it')\n   101\t    refuse_links_engine(cfg)\n   102\t    refuse_seeded_ledger(args.views)\n   103\t    refuse_harness_ledger(args.views)\n   104\t    uni = universe.load(args.universe)\n   105\t    archive = model.Archive(args.archive)\n   106\t    day_runner = None\n   107\t    if args.workers > 1:\n   177\t- A further check the change does not need is offered in one line, not run. After the checks that prove a change pass, report and close.\n   178\t- To accept a code change, replay stored decisions through the code deterministically ($0); a sampler run proves nothing about code,\n   179\t  and a cached replay proves nothing about the model.\n   180\t- A re-sent request is the bytes production sends, not the archive's canonical JSON: `model.request_identity` sorts keys for the\n   181\t  archive, `model.call` sends the dict as built. Check a harness by comparing an answer's key order with a production answer's.\n   182\t- Before any paid run, `python3 scripts/preflight.py` replays the stored model answers and matching records of the archives and ledgers\n   183\t  it is given through the current code checks and prompts ($0; it skips rows an earlier run refused, so read what it says it skipped)\n   184\t  and prints, per check, the cases judged and the refusals with reasons (it does not yet replay the coverage-start reading's check:\n   185\t  `CURRENT_STATE.md`, open defects). A changed share or a refusal on a case a person would accept is\n   186\t  diagnosed before the run; a paid run is never the way a check or a prompt is first tried.\n   335\t  he approved this rule the same day, D391).\n   336\t- **A model call is a sampler.** Re-send a stored request before calling its answer a defect. Totals and acceptance come from at least\n   337\t  three samples beside a fresh control; a bar the control fails against itself judges nothing. A direct-read pass\n   338\t  (one send per article, every answer read beside its source; D238) locates mechanisms; its counts are one draw and are labelled so.\n   339\t- **A written log.** One row per arm: hypothesis, change, numbers against the control (regressions first), kept or not. Refuted arms and\n   340\t  your own mistakes stay in it; the next session starts from its last row.\n   341\t- **Own the loop, within what Alex agreed.** When the direction is agreed and no stop is in force (`CURRENT_STATE.md`), push your own\n   342\t  hypotheses through $0 checks and probes of a few cents on stored cases without asking. A registered test, an end-to-end run, or\n   343\t  anything that departs from the agreed plan starts on his word; a result that contradicts the hypothesis stops the line and goes to him\n   344\t  (D355: \"如果有什么不符合hypothesis 的地方你需要终止然后和我说我们重新评估\"). While he is not active (D439), \"his word\" is the council's\n   345\t  recorded consensus, and a stopped line goes to the council for re-assessment.\n   346\t- **Council before every change is sent.** A prompt, schema, code or criterion change, including one found by reading a probe, is a new\n   347\t  arm. It goes to an independent Codex read and a challenge round before its first send, and its result goes to a challenge round\n   348\t  before the next change. Reading the traces and the model's own account locates a fix; it does not review it (Alex, 2026-11-08:\n   349\t  \"你这些改动有我你有好好的跑council 再下手\").\n   350\t  Every round's reasoning and decisions are reflected on in the council until there is a consensus; the decision is recorded, and\n   351\t  the next test goes straight ahead, held to the PRD and its quantified goals, with every trace read by you yourself and not only\n   352\t  by readers (Alex, 2026-11-10: \"你应该跑council loop 来reflect 你每一轮的思路还有决策，需要你打成共识，记录决策，向前直行测试。不要drift,我们有PRD和量化目标，\n   353\t  你自己作为llm 下去看所有trace\").\n   354\t- **Small first, then one run.** A hypothesis is first validated the simplest way, on a few stored cases and a few cents, and shown to\n   355\t  work at a high level before any full analysis or stability test is built (Alex, 2026-11-04: \"don't overspend resources on wasting time.\n   356\t  We need to validate the hypothesis in a very simple way and to make sure that it works on a high level before we dive into do the full\n   357\t  analysis stability test. This is a principle you should remember\"). A day or week slice before any whole-corpus run; preflight on\n   358\t  stored data before any paid run (§5); no edit while a run is live (§9). A paid run measures a configuration; it never tries a fix.\n   359\t  Before each send, write what it can still change in the decision and which risk the cheaper check left uncovered; a replicate or\n  6459\t- **The census** (`docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-selection/DEPENDENCY-SELECTION-CENSUS.md`): over the fifteen\n  6460\t  questions the old system opened in the stored unseeded fresh week, 97 pairs on which a dependency could have been chosen (each\n  6461\t  new question against the questions it was compared with and its same-article siblings). Claude's own read written first, then\n  6462\t  two blind readers of different model families (the same mark on 68 of 97; both mark \"the new question would want the earlier\n  6463\t  one's conclusion\" on 11, three of them strong). One clear case: data-center revenue FY2027 against total revenue FY2027 (the\n  6464\t  matching engine recorded \"part of\", 1.00): the total's conclusion carried the year's consensus ($406.05 billion from 09-23,\n  6465\t  about $412 billion from 09-25) and the $108 billion guidance (from 09-26), none of them anywhere in the data-center question's\n  6466\t  seven requests; its last answer names the gap and adds a step of its own. Negative controls: the Hugging Face commitments\n  6467\t  question (\"none\" in all three readings); a weakly granted pair whose line would carry a judge's own assumed numbers. The\n  6468\t  strongest driver relations of the week (Rubin shipping against the revenue questions) run from a later question to an earlier\n  6469\t  one and cannot be chosen at creation (D191 point 2, decided, unbuilt).\n  6470\t- **The council's corrections:** the $96.2 billion quarter was already in all seven requests (not a figure the line would\n  6471\t  supply); the one \"same question\" pair is one unordered pair; the line would not by itself make the answer supported.\n  6472\t- **Decided, citing D439:** the pair is a positive lead for the decided dependency-selection slice (D368 item 2), not a\n  6473\t  validation of it. Before the council reviews any six-call send on it: a bounded proof on stored data of what the edge would do\n  6474\t  to filing and to judging (a statement linked to both questions, the direction, ordering, cycle rejection, what the judge's\n  6475\t  request would contain, the unrelated pair as a negative case), with no frozen file changed. Stop, and return the conflict to\n  6476\t  the council as a design question, if the edge makes filing discard a home contrary to the governing rule, creates a cycle,\n  6477\t  cannot be represented without conflating the two meanings of the edge, or fails to show the intended conclusion.\n 10504\t\n 10505\t## D699 · 2026-11-21 · Alex continues with A: the whole-window measurement of the linking version, designed with the council at $0 first\n 10506\t\n 10507\t- **Alex's words** (2026-11-21), replying to the next-step question after D698 (A, recommended: run poc-v119 over 07-01..09-20 once,\n 10508\t  about $9 as one poc-v118 run cost, its measurement designed with the council at $0 before any send: whether related number questions\n 10509\t  still contradict each other, which links the matcher chose and whether any is wrong, whether the self-made market reference becomes\n 10510\t  rarer, whether other questions get worse; then decide whether the new version replaces the product. B: stop, poc-v118 stays): \"continue\".\n 10511\t- **Decided (Alex):** A. The $0 design and its council come first. The ~$9 was an estimate, not a ceiling: the paid send waits for the\n 10512\t  council's pass and a ceiling Alex names. poc-v118 stays the product until a later decision.\n 10513\t- **Next:** the design (`docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/`), its $0 proofs, council rounds, then the ceiling question to Alex.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python3 -c 'import json,sqlite3\nfrom pathlib import Path\nbase=Path(\\\"../../../poc/data/july-link-test-2026-11-21/linked-1/archive.sqlite\\\").resolve()\na=sqlite3.connect(base.as_uri()+\\\"?mode=ro&immutable=1\\\",uri=True)\nreq=json.loads(a.execute(\\\"select request_json from model_calls where call_id=8738\\\").fetchone()[0])\nfor v in (\\\"v1\\\",\\\"v2\\\",\\\"v8\\\",\\\"v9\\\"):\n print(req[\\\"questions\\\"][f\\\"reads__{v}\\\"][\\\"instructions\\\"])\nprint(req[\\\"state\\\"][:2800])'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python3 -c 'import json,sqlite3\nfrom pathlib import Path\nbase=Path(\\\"../../../poc/data/july-link-test-2026-11-21/linked-1/archive.sqlite\\\").resolve()\na=sqlite3.connect(base.as_uri()+\\\"?mode=ro&immutable=1\\\",uri=True)\nreq=json.loads(a.execute(\\\"select request_json from model_calls where call_id=8738\\\").fetchone()[0])\nfor v in (\\\"v1\\\",\\\"v2\\\",\\\"v8\\\",\\\"v9\\\"):\n print(req[\\\"questions\\\"][f\\\"reads__{v}\\\"][\\\"instructions\\\"])\nprint(req[\\\"state\\\"][:2800])'\"","aggregated_output":"Should one of these two questions be judged with the other's current conclusion in view? Use only the supplied text. THESIS:\nv1 (open, NVDA, asset, focus \"The amount of NVIDIA's credit-support obligations under the AI-cloud revenue-sharing and credit-support model.\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed under its AI-cloud model by the publication of its FY2027 annual report?\n    Ladder: Strongly bullish: Committed credit support is below $1 billion. / Bullish: Committed credit support is at least $1 billion and below $5 billion. / Neutral: Committed credit support is at least $5 billion and below $10 billion. / Bearish: Committed credit support is at least $10 billion and below $20 billion. / Strongly bearish: Committed credit support is at least $20 billion.; research group: g1 \"NVIDIA AI-cloud revenue-sharing and credit-support model\"\n    Evidence: [NVIDIA Corporation 2026-07-01] This new model enables AI clouds to procure NVIDIA infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise and ISV customers through economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model. Through the partnership, AI clouds will sell NVIDIA-powered cloud services, with NVIDIA earning both standard product revenue and a share of the cloud revenue on the supported capacity. This structure accelerates a\n    Evidence: [qz.com 2026-07-01] Nvidia $NVDA announced a revenue-sharing program on Wednesday designed to give AI startups, model builders, and enterprises access to its computing infrastructure without requiring them to finance hardware buildouts themselves.\nShould one of these two questions be judged with the other's current conclusion in view? Use only the supplied text. THESIS:\nv2 (open, NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA's credit support and capacity backstop obligations to customers and cloud providers\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): At NVIDIA's FY2027 annual report, how much will its commitments for customer and cloud-provider credit support, guarantees, and capacity backstops reach?\n    Ladder: Strongly bullish: Total commitments are less than $2 billion. / Bullish: Total commitments are at least $2 billion and less than $5 billion. / Neutral: Total commitments are at least $5 billion and less than $10 billion. / Bearish: Total commitments are at least $10 billion and less than $20 billion. / Strongly bearish: Total commitments are at least $20 billion.; research group: g2 \"NVIDIA AI Compute Partnership\", g1 \"NVIDIA AI-cloud revenue-sharing and credit-support model\"\n    Evidence: [moomoo.com 2026-07-01] On July 1, according to the technology news outlet The Information, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young cloud service providers that lease or rent its GPUs—if these companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent their computing capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price.\n    Evidence: [moomoo.com 2026-07-01] On July 1, NVIDIA published a statement on its official website announcing a new business model that combines revenue sharing with credit support, enabling AI cloud providers to procure NVIDIA infrastructure without bearing the full upfront capital expenditure, and allowing them to offer computing services to downstream AI-native enterprises, model developers, and corporate clients.\nShould one of these two questions be judged with the other's current conclusion in view? Use only the supplied text. THESIS:\nv8 (open, NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA stand-alone CPU sales\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much revenue will NVIDIA generate from stand-alone CPU sales in FY2027?\n    Ladder: Strongly bullish: Stand-alone CPU sales exceed $25 billion in FY2027. / Bullish: Stand-alone CPU sales are at least $20 billion and no more than $25 billion in FY2027. / Neutral: Stand-alone CPU sales are at least $15 billion and less than $20 billion in FY2027. / Bearish: Stand-alone CPU sales are at least $10 billion and less than $15 billion in FY2027. / Strongly bearish: Stand-alone CPU sales are less than $10 billion in FY2027.\n    Evidence: [fool.com (analyst) ] Intel has led here for years and holds more than 59% of total CPU market share\n    Evidence: [fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-05] AMD has gained ground, progressing from a low of about 17% back in 2016 to 38% today.\nShould one of these two questions be judged with the other's current conclusion in view? Use only the supplied text. THESIS:\nv9 (open, NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA FY2027 Data Center segment revenue\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\n    Ladder: Strongly bullish: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $450 billion. / Bullish: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $400 billion and below $450 billion. / Neutral: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $350 billion and below $400 billion. / Bearish: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $300 billion and below $350 billion. / Strongly bearish: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is below $300 billion.\n    Evidence: [tradingview.com ] During the first quarter of fiscal 2027, the Data Center segment generated $75.25 billion in revenues, accounting for 92% of total sales. Revenues from the segment surged 92% year over year and grew 21% sequentially, driven by the rapid ramp-up of Blackwell 300 systems and robust networking demand.\n    Evidence: [NVIDIA 2026-07-07] NVIDIA is collaborating with Nokia to deliver AI-RAN platforms integrated with Nokia anyRAN software for AI-native 5G-Advanced networks ready for 6G evolution.\nCANDIDATE (asset, object NVDA, focus \"NVIDIA FY2027 total revenue\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much total revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\nKey: measure=total_revenue, period_start=2026-02-01, period_end=2027-01-31, unit=USD\nLadder: Strongly bullish: Total revenue is at least $450 billion. / Bullish: Total revenue is at least $400 billion but less than $450 billion. / Neutral: Total revenue is at least $350 billion but less than $400 billion. / Bearish: Total revenue is at least $300 billion but less than $350 billion. / Strongly bearish: Total revenue is less than $300 billion.\nIts evidence: [Jeran Wittenstein, citing data compiled by Bloomberg ] The chipmaker is projected to deliver $228 billion in profits on sales of $393 billion in fiscal 2027, which ends Jan. 31. That would represent an expansion of 90% and 82%, respectively, according to data compiled by Bloomberg.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
