{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"agent_message","text":"**NOT READY.** The launcher and store protections are fixed, and the $0 replay proves a complete dry draw. The scorer can still pass with zero whole-window draws, the registration rules out some links the prompt permits, and the cited preflight covered only the July prefix for matching.\n\n## 1. Round-13 fixes\n\n- **Gate and completeness — partly fixed.** The dry run records 105 proposals, zero written links and `complete: True`. The launcher checks READS coverage, conditional SIGN coverage and answered jobs. But `read_census.py` reads the stores directly, skips missing answers and never checks that three distinct draws completed successfully. I ran it with no census folders. It printed `0 census, 3 July` and **PASS**. I also checked that changing the known positive’s sign to `opposite` still counts it as right. Require three distinct, successfully completed whole-window draws, reject missing answers, and check the sign for the known positive. Add both cases to the $0 checks. [Dry-run record](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/census-dry.log:65) · [Completion check](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/window_test.py:135) · [Scorer](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/read_census.py:47)\n- **July samples — reused in scoring.** The scorer includes the three stored answers at seq 148. Round 13 verified that both the READS and SIGN request hashes match. The paid callback would still buy fresh answers at that opening. I recommend replaying the corresponding July sample in each of the three draws unless the design states what three additional samples could change. [July reuse](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/read_census.py:61) · [Paid callback](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/window_test.py:67) · [AGENTS §7.3](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/AGENTS.md:354)\n- **Ceiling, crash handling and store isolation — fixed.** The launcher checks for a finite positive ceiling, requires the ledger to match it, and registers its lock before enabling transport. Replay marks each store harness-only before routing and holds the store lock through the pass. For sequential draws, the **estimated** $0.066 settlement plus one $0.0084 reservation gives about **$0.0744**, within a $0.10 ceiling. A budget stop still leaves an incomplete census. Alex’s ceiling remains required. [Store marker and lock](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/window_test.py:49) · [Census launcher](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/window_test.py:170) · [Price estimate](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/DESIGN.md:117) · [D699](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:10505)\n- **Preflight — open.** The cited log covers all 1,884 extraction answers, but checks 61 matching records and skips one. I counted 62 matching records through seq 147 and 1,287 in the finished old-2 store. Run and record preflight against the full-window ledger and archive. The replay equality check complements that preflight. [Cited preflight](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/preflight.log:5) · [Preflight entry point](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/scripts/preflight.py:326) · [AGENTS §5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/AGENTS.md:182)\n\n## 2. Registered links\n\nThe JSON covers exactly the 105 eligible pairs. **The Data Center positive stands.** D537 traces what the segment lacked and what the company-wide answer would supply. The CPU-to-company links need a narrower claim. At seq 148, the request shows about $20B in stand-alone CPU sales against $393B in total revenue. The total contains the part, but its upper bound is too distant to establish that reading it is required. The same concern applies to total CPU revenue at seq 265. I would move both to O unless the supplied evidence establishes a useful constraint or driver. The SIGN criterion itself allows abstention when a cap sits far above the likely answer. [Registered CPU pairs](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/expected-links.md:11) · [July request archive, call 8738](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/july-link-test-2026-11-21/linked-1/archive.sqlite) · [SIGN criterion](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/links.py:32) · [D537](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:6463)\n\n**The blanket financing W rule is too broad.** In the seq-148 request, the v1 evidence explicitly says NVIDIA’s credit support enables infrastructure purchases and yields product revenue and a share of cloud revenue. That gives `v14 reads v1` a defensible driver under the second READS clause. I would mark that pair O and check the other financing W pairs for the same kind of supplied connection before freezing the labels. [Registered W pair](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/expected-links.md:13) · [July request archive, call 8738](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/july-link-test-2026-11-21/linked-1/archive.sqlite) · [READS criterion](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/links.py:22)\n\nRegistering **part reads whole** is sound for the established Data Center case. Applying it as an exclusive rule for every part–whole pair goes beyond D537 and the current prompt. Keep the known positive, and label the remaining directions from the material each pair was shown. Also remove §3’s earlier claim that both directions have governing sources. Its later correction is right about the historical seed rule. [Design’s two source readings](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/DESIGN.md:78) · [Design’s scope correction](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/DESIGN.md:94) · [PRD 06](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/prd/06-views.md:134)\n\nBefore sending, make the scorer require three complete draws and the correct sign on the known positive, correct the registration and rerun its $0 scoring checks, and record full-window preflight. The sequential budget plan can then go to Alex for the ceiling.\n\n**NOT READY**"}}
